# Environment variables — never commit secrets.
#
# Ignore EVERY `.env*` file rather than listing the three names we happen to
# use today. The previous list (`.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.*.local`) left a
# real gap: `.env.production`, `.env.development` and `.env.test` match none of
# those patterns, so creating one — which is ordinary Next.js practice and
# something a tool or a future contributor may do without thinking — would have
# silently staged real secrets for commit. A deny-all-then-allow-back list is
# the right shape here, because the cost of the two errors is wildly asymmetric:
# an ignored file that should have been tracked is noticed in seconds, while a
# committed secret is in the history forever and has to be rotated.
.env*

# ...except a committed template of VARIABLE NAMES ONLY, never values. Nothing
# matching this name may ever contain a real credential.
!.env.example

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output
.next/
dist/
build/
.expo/
.turbo/

# OS/editor
.DS_Store
